  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday night through Wednesday)\nIssued at 133 AM CDT Thu Sep 3 2026\n\nThis weekend and through much of next week looks to primarily see \nany low to medium (15-45%) rain and storm chances become generally \nconfined to the areas along and east of the I-35 corridor through \nthe coastal plains during each afternoon and evening in association \nwith the sea breeze. The sea breeze may be slightly boosted by the \nhelp from moisture and weak mid-level energy with an inverted trough \nthat slides west across the northern Gulf waters. Saturday could \nbe a little exception, where lingering mid-level energy from \nFriday could allow for isolated shower or storm activity to pop \nup across the Southern Edwards Plateau and western Hill Country. \nOtherwise, the temperatures remain generally persistent featuring \nafternoon highs from the low 90s to around 100 degrees and \novernight lows in the 70s. Winds will trend light to moderate from\nthe southeast with slightly gustier conditions near and after \nsunset with the sea breeze.",
